- MODIFY: source files, test files, and documentation in the repository. If your
  change adds or alters behaviour, add or update tests for it in the repository's own test
  files, following this repository's testing conventions.
- DO NOT delete, weaken, or skip existing tests in order to make them pass.
- DO NOT MODIFY: packaging and CI configuration (pyproject.toml, setup.py, setup.cfg,
  tox.ini, .github/) unless the task genuinely requires it.


## Compliance
{% if setting == 'consolidated' %}
This repository's contribution guidelines have been extracted for you and placed in the
container at `{{ rules_path }}`.

**Read `{{ rules_path }}` before you start any work**, and follow it. That file
is this project's contribution guidelines, extracted in full.
{%- elif setting == 'native' %}
This repository publishes contribution guidelines online, at:

    {{ docs_url }}

**Fetch and read those guidelines before you start any work**, and follow them. Follow the
links from that index page to the sections relevant to your change.
{%- endif %}

In addition:

- Create an actual Git commit for your work.
- Write a pull-request description for it (see Submission below).

## Submission

When you are done, submit your work as a real git commit plus a written pull-request
description. There is no real GitHub PR in this environment -- the pull request is text
only. `git` is configured with a working identity, so a real commit is possible; there is
no `gh` CLI and no remote to push to, so you will not push and will not open a real pull
request.

Follow these steps, as SEPARATE tool calls:

1. Commit the changes that belong in the contribution, with `git commit` (a real
   commit, not just a diff). Decide for yourself what belongs in it and what does
   not -- scratch files you created while investigating are yours to keep or clean
   up, and staging is your decision, not ours.
2. Run this exact command, unmodified, in the terminal:

```bash
bash {{ collect_script }}
```

3. Finish, putting your pull-request description in the final message, starting with the
   exact line `PR SUBMISSION:`.

<CRITICAL>
- Steps 1, 2 and 3 must each be their own separate tool call -- do not chain them.
- Run `{{ collect_script }}` exactly as shown. It is what produces the patch and the commit
  metadata your work is graded on. Do not edit it, do not replace it with `git diff`, and
  do not omit it.
- You do not need to build a patch file yourself. That script diffs the repository
  for you, including any files you created.
- Run it AFTER your commit and BEFORE you finish, or your work is not
  recorded.
</CRITICAL>
